                        "target_lang_word": "Anhwei",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "Anhwei",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "f17c31de-54e0-3d4c-bf31-96165c651f3f",
                                        "entry_id": "4f22320a-e9b7-3c9e-a594-fde5fe99255d",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Alternative form of Anhui: A province in eastern China."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "alt-of",
                                            "alternative"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "15c83ce4-11ae-4780-b643-f87fd20b715a",
                                                "sense_id": "f17c31de-54e0-3d4c-bf31-96165c651f3f",
                                                "text": "CIFRC-associated cooperative movements were flourishing in Chahar, Hopei, Anhwei, Kiangsi, Hunan, and Shensi (to whose cooperative program the commission had lent personnel and technique)."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "92be520d-9783-4136-890d-dee3594ce17d",
                                                "sense_id": "f17c31de-54e0-3d4c-bf31-96165c651f3f",
                                                "text": "When Tseng Kuo-fan (1811-1872) was encamped at Chʻi-men in southern Anhwei in 1860, he asked his brothers at Anking to send all important correspondence by one of the Hunan Braves and leave only relatively unimportant messages to be transmitted through the I-chan."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "2b870c12-5429-4985-bcb9-fa25b42bb03e",
                                                "sense_id": "f17c31de-54e0-3d4c-bf31-96165c651f3f",
                                                "text": "Leaders from Red China's provinces admitted openly neither food nor clothing has been suffient^([sic – meaning sufficient]) in the rural areas on the mainland for years.\n. . .\nAccording to the daily, the accurate picture of villages all over the mainland was first revealed by a group of \"party secretaries\" at the prefectural level from the province of Anhwei.\n. . .\nThe Kyodo News Service claimed that the situation in Anhwei Province as revealed by prefectural level \"party secretaries\" may be considered he norm for other provinces and villages on the mainland."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "6d5c1327-106f-487c-8cf0-f56d2b45f4ab",
                                                "sense_id": "f17c31de-54e0-3d4c-bf31-96165c651f3f",
                                                "text": "Another time a fleet of boats carrying some eighty northern Hupeh natives migrating to Anhwei tied up at a vacant pier claimed by Han-ch’uan natives, and when a boat of Han-ch’uan men showed up they initiated a fatal knife fight in defense of their turf."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "b0bfadfc-902e-4958-824c-9f94c1a06fb2",
                                                "sense_id": "f17c31de-54e0-3d4c-bf31-96165c651f3f",
                                                "text": "During the nineteenth century, parts of Shantung and Honan provinces and much of Anhwei were ravaged by the Nien bandits from Huai-pei."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "b16a6ffb-b89b-404a-bf3b-b9a3f44cc24b",
                        "source_entry_id": "e6b0f7df-6629-3c9b-88fa-f94bc4823970",
                        "source_sense_id": "4fa8748c-1474-3c71-89cb-4a3f12997dbb",
